[Music]  videos posted by social media users  reportedly show a military drill in ping  tang in the fijian province of china  separately this footage of a rocket  being launched was released today by  chinese state media the drills come as  beijing expressed their anger at the u.s  house speaker nancy pelosi's visit to  taiwan earlier this week  well let's get military analysis on the  situation now joining me now is retired  marshal ed stringer who was also the  ministry of defense's director of  operations ed it's great to have you  here now china currently holding six  drills in these um six and holding  drills in these six areas are we likely  to expect any further escalation at all  no i think the first thing to say is  this is a diplomatic crisis this has  been caused by speaker pelosi making  a visit by the most senior american  politician for four decades  and china's using its military  instrument here and that dramatic  footage that you've just shown  to signal its  deep  regret and concern  about about that visit and whether that  questions this nuanced balance that we  refer to as strategic ambiguity that all  parties have have maintained over the  last couple of decades so i don't see it  being a military escalation escalate to  what to an invasion or a blockade which  clearly those six areas ringing taiwan  are supposed to suggest an ability for  china to do if it wished to but a  blockade or an invasion are very  significant very difficult military  operations that you have to plan and  prepare for and we haven't seen that and  even if they conduct them they would  carry a huge amount of operational and  political risk so i don't see this  escalating strategically in the military  dimension and how does china's military  equipment then compare to other  developed nations  well that's uh that's a good question  the answer is we don't really know and  the reason being is that over past  decades china has relied on the soviet  union and then russia to provide it with  the bulk of its military equipment and  then it went through a phase of copying  that military military equipment and and  building its own so here you see uh the  russian flanker the su-27 series and  there is the chinese j-16 you know a  literal copy  but then as china has evolved and  remember china's been involved in  decades worth of um  industrial espionage against the west  stealing a lot of our technology it's  become a a genuine scientific and  manufacturing superpower and now it's  overtaken russia and its ability to  build high-tech military equipment so  here you see uh the j-20 clearly an  answer to the american  f-22 fighter and as you can see we  talked about the theft of of technology  and designs from the west well those  look suspiciously similar don't they and  if you look in the maritime environment  as well though these look quite similar  on the left there  a type 52 was built very much with  russian increasingly soviet weapons  weapons sorry increasingly chinese  weapons systems here the much more  advanced stealthy type 55 which is very  highly regarded by the west is a chinese  design and carries chinese weapons  systems but i think the point to be made  is that's a very steep learning curve  for china and when you're on a steep  learning curve you can make mistakes  they haven't been tested in combat and  china has not actually fought a war  since the 1970s so we do not know how  they would perform  conducting those sort of complex  operations that we've seen china earth  sorry russia failed to manage in ukraine  absolutely ed thank you very much ed  stringer there

